    "turn_text": "This BBC podcast is supported by ads outside the UK. Welcome to HardTalk on the BBC World Service with me, Stephen Sacker. For this interview recorded on the 7th of September, I'm at the Ambrosetti Forum, an annual gathering of international politicians in northern Italy. Attendees have flown in from all corners of the world to discuss a range of political and economic challenges, but all have an interest in one simple question. Who will win November's U.S. presidential election? Will Donald Trump make a dramatic return to the White House? Or will Vice President Kamala Harris become America's first female president? The polls are tight, while Trump appeared on course for victory over Joe Biden. His withdrawal and the uncontested nomination of Kamala Harris has re-energized the Democratic Party and raised questions about Trump's appeal to key swing voters. My guest is close ally and sometime confidant of Donald Trump, Republican Senator from South Carolina, Lindsey Graham. He's campaigning hard for Trump, portraying Harris as a grave danger to both America and the wider world. But is that a winning message? Well, Senator Graham joins me now. Welcome to our talk. Thank you. Senator, why has the replacement of Joe Biden with Kamala Harris on the Democratic side of this presidential race? Why has it knocked the Trump campaign so badly off-balance?",
